Chapter: RevisedNarrow
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 __narrow through revised.

 A revised field's value is hoisted into a let, because __record-set
 mutates in place and every value must be computed before the first
 store. That hoist used to swallow the __narrow: the checker suppresses
 CDX2051 when it can SEE __narrow at the store, and after hoisting it saw
 a bare name. So the identical assertion passed in a record literal and
 was rejected in a revised update, and the diagnostic recommended the
 very thing the author had already written.

 Both forms below assert the same thing about the same value. Both must
 compile, and both must trap on the same out-of-range value at runtime --
 the assertion has to still be an assertion, not merely a way to quiet
 the checker.

 The counterpart is errors/revised-narrow-absent: without __narrow, the
 same store is still CDX2051. If that probe ever passes, this fix has
 turned the hatch into a hole.

Section: Types

  Reading = record {
   level : Integer between 0 and 100,
   label : Text
  }

Section: The Two Forms

 The record literal: the form that always worked.

  make-literal : Integer -> Reading
  make-literal (n) = Reading { level = __narrow n, label = "lit" }

 The revised update: the form that did not.

  make-revised : Reading, Integer -> Reading
  make-revised (r) (n) = r revised { level = __narrow n, label = "rev" }

 More than one field, only one of them narrowed, and the narrowed one
 second -- the per-field decision has to hold whatever order they come
 in.

  make-mixed : Reading, Integer -> Reading
  make-mixed (r) (n) = r revised { label = "mixed", level = __narrow n }

Section: Body

 Each line gets its own Reading, and that is not tidiness. revised is a
 __record-set chain and __record-set mutates in place, returning the same
 record -- so `let b = make-revised a 7` would leave a and b the same
 object, and a later revise of b would rewrite what a reads. The first
 cut of this test threaded one record through all three and printed the
 last value three times. Same lesson as list-set-at and ttt-copy-squares:
 a caller that re-reads the old value must not hand its record to a
 revise.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let a = make-literal 42
   in let b = make-revised (make-literal 5) 7
   in let c = make-mixed (make-literal 5) 99
   in act
    print-line-uni ("literal: " & show (a.level) & " " & (a.label))
    print-line-uni ("revised: " & show (b.level) & " " & (b.label))
    print-line-uni ("mixed: " & show (c.level) & " " & (c.label))
   end
  end
